course: geography
lesson: 30
title: The Atlantic routes and the slave trade
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A sailing ship could not go straight wherever it liked: it had to follow the winds. Across the Atlantic ships went out with the trade winds in the south and came home with the westerlies in the north, a great loop linking Europe, Africa and the Americas.
explain: Along that loop ran one of the worst crimes in history. Ships carried cloth, guns and metal to West Africa, carried enslaved Africans across the ocean to the Americas, and brought back sugar, tobacco and cotton grown by enslaved people.
explain: About 12.5 million Africans were forced onto ships, and about 2 million died on the crossing, called the Middle Passage, chained below deck in crowded, filthy holds for weeks. A famous drawing of the slave ship Brookes in 1788 showed this and helped turn British opinion against the trade.
explain: Enslaved people resisted all the time: revolts on ships, running away, free maroon communities like Palmares in Brazil, and the Haitian Revolution, which made Haiti independent in 1804.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 colonies send sugar and tobacco as trade goods and the game ends in 1836. Behind every such good were real people forced to work, and slavery went on after the game ends: in the USA until 1865 and in Brazil until 1888.
fact: Sailing ships crossed the Atlantic in a loop, going out with the trade winds in the south and coming back with the westerlies in the north.
fact: Ships sailed from Europe to West Africa with goods such as cloth, guns and metal, carried enslaved Africans to the Americas, and brought back sugar, tobacco and cotton, which is called the triangular trade.
fact: Ports such as Liverpool, Bristol, Nantes and Lisbon grew rich from this trade.
fact: About 12.5 million Africans were forced onto ships across the Atlantic between the 1500s and the 1860s, and about 2 million died on the voyage.
fact: Most enslaved Africans were taken to Brazil and the Caribbean, and fewer than half a million directly to North America; Brazil alone took more than any other country.
fact: The crossing from Africa to the Americas was called the Middle Passage, and people were chained below deck in crowded, filthy conditions for weeks.
fact: The slave trade caused wars, raids and the loss of millions of young people from West and Central Africa, and in 1526 King Afonso I of Kongo wrote to the king of Portugal complaining about it.
fact: Enslaved people resisted in many ways, from revolts on ships to running away and building free communities.
fact: Escaped enslaved people formed free communities called maroon settlements in Jamaica, Suriname, Brazil and elsewhere, and Palmares in Brazil lasted for most of the 1600s; they were often hidden in mountains and forests, where soldiers found them hard to reach.
fact: The Haitian Revolution from 1791 overthrew slavery in the French colony of Saint-Domingue, and Haiti became independent in 1804.
fact: Britain banned its slave trade in 1807 and slavery in most of its colonies from 1834, while slavery lasted in the USA until 1865 and in Brazil until 1888.
fact: When Britain ended slavery, it paid £20 million to slave owners for their "lost property" and nothing to the people who had been enslaved.
fact: Tens of millions of people in the Americas today, in Brazil, the USA, the Caribbean and elsewhere, are descendants of Africans brought by the slave trade, and music such as jazz, samba and reggae grew partly from the music they brought from Africa.
exercise: How did sailing ships cross the Atlantic and come back? | Out with the trade winds in the south, back with the westerlies in the north | Out and back along exactly the same line, the shortest way both times | Out with the westerlies in the north, back with the monsoon winds of the south | Straight across, rowing against the wind
exercise: What did ships carry from Europe to West Africa? | Cloth, guns and metal | Sugar, tobacco and cotton | Gold and ivory from the Swahili coast | Silver and silk from China
exercise: What did ships bring back to Europe from the Americas? | Sugar, tobacco and cotton | Cloth, guns and metal goods | Spices from the Moluccas | Furs from Siberia
exercise: What is the three-part route of this trade called? | The triangular trade | The Silk Road of the West | The Columbian Exchange | The Roaring Forties route
exercise: About how many Africans were forced onto ships across the Atlantic? | About 12.5 million | About 12,500 | About 125 million | About 1.25 billion
exercise: About how many died on the voyage? | About 2 million | About 200 people | Hardly anyone at all | Almost all of them
exercise: Where were most enslaved Africans taken? | To Brazil and the Caribbean | To North America and Canada | To the ports of Europe | To China and India
exercise: What was the Middle Passage? | The crossing from Africa to the Americas | The road over the mountains from Spain to France | The strait between Africa and Arabia | The route home from the Americas to Europe
exercise: Which port grew rich from the slave trade? | Liverpool | Samarkand | Calicut | Kilwa
exercise: What did the slave trade do to West and Central Africa? | Caused wars, raids and the loss of millions of young people | Made every African kingdom richer, stronger and more peaceful | Had no effect on Africa at all | Brought peace between all African states
exercise: What were maroon settlements? | Free communities of people who had escaped slavery | Plantations owned by the kings of Spain and Portugal | Forts built by the Portuguese on the African coast | Ports where sugar was loaded
exercise: Which maroon community lasted for most of the 1600s in Brazil? | Palmares | Potosí | Malacca | Port Royal
exercise: Which country became independent in 1804 after enslaved people freed themselves? | Haiti | Brazil | Jamaica | Barbados
exercise: When did Britain ban its slave trade? | In 1807 | In 1492 | In 1888 | In 1600
exercise: When did slavery end in the USA? | In 1865 | In 1807 | In 1776 | In 1888
exercise: Who received money when Britain ended slavery in its colonies? | The slave owners | The people who had been enslaved | The African kings who had sold captives | The sailors of the Royal Navy
exercise: Which was the last country in the Americas to end slavery? | Brazil, in 1888 | The USA, in 1865 | Britain, in 1834 | Haiti, in 1804
test: My mate says ships just sailed straight there and straight back. Why did they take different routes? | They followed the trade winds out and the westerlies back | Pirates guarded the straight route in both directions all year | The straight route was too short | Captains liked to see new places
test: Someone says "enslaved people just accepted it". Put that right. | They resisted, with revolts, escapes and free communities | It is right, nobody ever resisted slavery in any way | Only people in Europe ever fought against slavery | They resisted only after 1888
test: Why did so many people die on the Middle Passage? | They were chained in crowded, filthy holds for weeks | The ships nearly always sank in the Atlantic storms | The crossing took only one day | They were given too much food
test: In the game, sugar from a Caribbean colony is a trade good. What was behind it? | The forced labour of enslaved Africans | Paid farmers from Britain | Machines that did all the work | Sugar that grew wild with no work
test: The game ends in 1836. Was slavery over everywhere by then? | No, it lasted in the USA until 1865 and Brazil until 1888 | Yes, it ended everywhere in 1807, when Britain banned the trade | Yes, Haiti ended it for the whole world | No, it never ended anywhere
test: Why were maroon communities often in mountains or forests? | Rough land was hard for soldiers to reach | The soil there was best for sugar | Owners sent them there on purpose | Mountains were warmer than the coast
test: A drawing of the ship Brookes was published in 1788. Why did it matter? | It showed how people were packed and turned opinion against the trade | It was an advert in the newspapers to sell more ships to Liverpool merchants | It showed a new kind of sail | It was a map of the Atlantic winds
test: Why do so many Brazilians have African ancestors? | Brazil received more enslaved Africans than anywhere else | Brazil is joined to Africa by land, so people walked there | Most Africans moved there by choice in the 1950s | Brazil was ruled by African kings
test: Britain paid £20 million when it ended slavery. Who got nothing? | The people who had been enslaved | The slave owners | The British government | The ports of Liverpool and Bristol
test: How did the slave trade harm Africa, not only the people taken? | It brought wars and raids and drained kingdoms like Kongo | It made every African kingdom grow larger and richer | It only affected the Americas, never Africa itself | It ended all trade with Africa
test: Music like jazz, samba and reggae has roots in which part of the world? | Africa, brought by the people of the slave trade | Medieval Venice and the music of its gondoliers | China, carried west along the Silk Road | Northern Russia
exam: Identify the principal reason for which vessels crossing the Atlantic followed different routes outward and homeward. | They exploited the trade winds outward and the westerlies homeward | Customs regulations forbade the return by the outward route | The homeward route was invariably obstructed by ice | The outward route was shorter only in the summer months
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Middle Passage? | The crossing in which enslaved Africans were transported, in appalling conditions, to the Americas | The return voyage of merchant vessels laden with sugar and tobacco from the Americas to Europe | The strait separating the continent of Africa from Arabia | A route of the overland trade between China and Persia
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the compensation paid upon the abolition of slavery in the British colonies. | Twenty million pounds was paid to the slave owners and nothing to the enslaved | Twenty million pounds was paid to the formerly enslaved persons | The sum was divided equally between owners and the enslaved | No payment whatsoever was made to any party
exam: It is given that a free state was established in 1804 by persons who had overthrown slavery in a French colony. Ascertain the state thus described. | Haiti | Jamaica | Brazil | Barbados
